WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.900
- [Tutor] We've added
the ability for our enemy

2
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.866
to spawn at random
positions on our screen.

3
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.933
Now, we're going to do just
a bit more code clean up

4
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:22.033
and teach you something new about

5
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:24.200
creating your own custom methods.

6
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:30.066
In our spawn manager script,
we have in our Start method,

7
00:00:30.066 --> 00:00:35.066
a way to create a position
for each of our enemies.

8
00:00:35.833 --> 00:00:40.133
Now, in the past we've made our
own custom functions before,

9
00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:42.266
so we're going to do that again.

10
00:00:42.266 --> 00:00:47.100
Now, we're going to make this
a private method, so that way,

11
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.100
we only need to use this
method inside of this class,

12
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.400
so we don't need it anywhere else really.

13
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.900
For now, we're going to
make this a void method,

14
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.900
so it doesn't really do
anything, except tell us things.

15
00:00:59.933 --> 00:01:02.866
We're going to call it
GenerateSpawnPosition.

16
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.866
And then we can just leave the parentheses

17
00:01:10.866 --> 00:01:14.000
because we're not actually
passing it any information,

18
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.166
it's just going to do something.

19
00:01:16.166 --> 00:01:18.866
So now, in our GenerateSpawnPosition,

20
00:01:18.866 --> 00:01:22.233
we're actually just
going to use this code,

21
00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:26.233
so that we can create
our own spawn position.

22
00:01:26.233 --> 00:01:30.766
So if we control or
command x, and we paste it

23
00:01:30.766 --> 00:01:33.100
into our GenerateSpawnPosition.

24
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.666
So now you can see our random
position is mad at us for now,

25
00:01:35.666 --> 00:01:36.833
but that's fine.

26
00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:41.600
So what this is gonna do, is
enter GenerateSpawnPosition.

27
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.466
When we call this in our
scripts and our update,

28
00:01:44.466 --> 00:01:46.500
it'll create a random
spawn position for us.

29
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.733
So it'll create this random X position,

30
00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:51.400
it'll create this random Z position.

31
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.766
And then, it will store those numbers

32
00:01:54.766 --> 00:01:57.533
into a random position, which is great,

33
00:01:57.533 --> 00:02:00.866
except the problem is, it
doesn't actually do anything

34
00:02:00.866 --> 00:02:03.800
from there, it just does this calculation,

35
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.566
gives us a random coordinate
and then it doesn't actually

36
00:02:06.566 --> 00:02:09.100
do anything with the coordinate.

37
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:14.100
Now, this is a key difference
between using void functions,

38
00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.733
and return type functions.

39
00:02:16.733 --> 00:02:20.800
So a void function, you
use for a specific tasks

40
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.733
that you do not need to return a value.

41
00:02:23.733 --> 00:02:27.066
For example, in one of
our previous lessons,

42
00:02:27.066 --> 00:02:32.066
we actually used a spawn
method using the void keyword.

43
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.600
Now in that case, we just wanted to create

44
00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.366
a random object on the
screen and that's all that

45
00:02:38.366 --> 00:02:39.466
that method did.

46
00:02:39.466 --> 00:02:44.266
However, when we actually use
methods that return a value,

47
00:02:44.266 --> 00:02:47.033
we do it for the purpose
of giving us information

48
00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:48.733
after a calculation.

49
00:02:48.733 --> 00:02:52.500
So GenerateSpawnPosition,
actually gives us a position

50
00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.333
that we need to use.

51
00:02:54.333 --> 00:02:59.000
So, we take out the void keyword
and we put in the specific

52
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.166
type of data that we want
to come out of this method.

53
00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:06.566
In this case, we go from
a void to a Vector3.

54
00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:12.633
Now, you can see that
our program is mad at us

55
00:03:12.633 --> 00:03:16.166
because this doesn't return a value.

56
00:03:16.166 --> 00:03:18.133
Fortunately, we have a value here,

57
00:03:18.133 --> 00:03:21.400
we have a Vector3 that we've already made.

58
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.700
But now, we need this
Vector3 to actually come out

59
00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:28.400
and result from this method.

60
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.566
So, if we actually type
in the keyword return,

61
00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.400
we can return our random position.

62
00:03:38.266 --> 00:03:42.900
So now, when we actually
go into our Start method,

63
00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.500
where we had the random position before,

64
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:49.933
we can actually now just call
generate random position.

65
00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:58.100
So now in our Instantiate method,

66
00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.900
it's no longer unhappy with us.

67
00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:04.866
That's because, in place
of our random position,

68
00:04:04.866 --> 00:04:07.533
when we call GenerateSpawnPosition,

69
00:04:07.533 --> 00:04:12.533
it actually outputs or
returns a random Vector3

70
00:04:14.233 --> 00:04:15.266
that we need.

71
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.866
So, in specific cases, where
we're actually trying to get

72
00:04:19.866 --> 00:04:23.666
some kind of data out of our methods,

73
00:04:23.666 --> 00:04:28.633
we can use return types, to
actually get that data back

74
00:04:28.633 --> 00:04:30.066
from that method.

75
00:04:30.066 --> 00:04:31.733
Now, it's a very confusing concept,

76
00:04:31.733 --> 00:04:34.933
the difference between Vector3's and voids

77
00:04:34.933 --> 00:04:36.700
but over time, as you use them more

78
00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:39.000
and understand how you're
supposed to use them,

79
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.166
it gets a lot easier.

80
00:04:42.633 --> 00:04:46.166
So now you can see when we go
into our game and press play,

81
00:04:49.266 --> 00:04:52.533
it spawns a random enemy
in a random position.

82
00:04:52.533 --> 00:04:55.633
If we press play again, it spawns it

83
00:04:55.633 --> 00:04:58.566
in another random position once again.

84
00:04:58.566 --> 00:05:03.166
So what you're gonna do, in
your spawn manager script,

85
00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:07.666
you're going to create
a new private method

86
00:05:08.866 --> 00:05:13.700
and instead of void, like you
can see in Update or Start,

87
00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:16.033
we're going to actually put Vector3

88
00:05:17.933 --> 00:05:20.700
and we're gonna call this
GenerateSpawnPosition.

89
00:05:21.566 --> 00:05:24.133
And then we take all the code that we use

90
00:05:24.133 --> 00:05:27.833
to generate random positions
on the X and the Z.

91
00:05:28.933 --> 00:05:32.833
We then take our vector
variable that we've created

92
00:05:32.833 --> 00:05:37.066
and then we use the word
return and we return

93
00:05:37.066 --> 00:05:40.200
that variable that we've made so that way

94
00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.400
when we use our instantiate method

95
00:05:43.866 --> 00:05:47.400
or GenerateSpawnPosition,
will actually give us

96
00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.966
a Vector3 that we can then use

97
00:05:49.966 --> 00:05:52.433
as part of this entire line of code.

98
00:05:53.733 --> 00:05:54.666
Now it's your turn.

99
00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:59.066
(upbeat sound)
